Use a working model run as the baseline. The [LLM example]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/build-and-run-with-bazel').ref('run-an-llm-example'))
accepts a model path and a fixed prompt and prints the selected runtime and
attention backend. Record those settings and the ZML revision with your results.

# [Select a build or profiling configuration]($heading.id('select-a-build-or-profiling-configuration'))

Build the target once with the flags you will use for the run. A first Bazel
invocation can fetch dependencies and compile the executable. Starting the
executable can still load weights and compile the model for the device.
Timing the entire command includes all of that work.

For a device trace, choose a Bazel configuration for your profiler, such as
`--config=nsys` for NVIDIA Nsight Systems or `--config=rocprofv3` for AMD ROCm.
The LLM example also accepts `--profile` with a non-interactive prompt to
capture a PJRT trace. The [profiling configuration reference]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/build-and-run-with-bazel').ref('select-a-build-or-profiling-configuration'))
lists the wrappers and the backends they enable.

When comparing two configurations, keep the model weights, input, device, and
build mode fixed. Compare startup separately from execution and use runs with
profiling disabled for elapsed-time comparisons. For a running LLMD HTTP
service, use [LLMD service metrics]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/operations/monitoring-and-troubleshooting').ref('scrape-service-metrics'))
to observe time to first token and generation throughput.
